WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.863
Parser robustness is about availability as well as correctness.

2
00:00:03.863 --> 00:00:13.978
Large payloads, deeply nested data, repeated structures, expensive
conversions, and complex YAML features can consume memory, CPU, time,
storage, or recursion depth.

3
00:00:13.978 --> 00:00:21.354
A parser that behaves correctly for small examples may still be unsafe if
it accepts unbounded input in a production service.

4
00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:24.585
Resource controls should be chosen near the boundary.

5
00:00:24.585 --> 00:00:37.791
API gateways, web frameworks, queues, file upload handlers, and
application code may all provide places to enforce request body limits,
file size limits, message size limits, timeout behavior, and nesting
expectations.

6
00:00:37.791 --> 00:00:43.621
The controls should be documented and tested so developers know where
oversized data is rejected.

7
00:00:43.621 --> 00:00:52.120
Streaming and incremental processing can be appropriate for intentionally
large JSON data, but they are design choices, not automatic fixes.

8
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.091
Streaming still needs validation strategy, error handling, partial failure
behavior, and limits.

9
00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:07.363
YAML is often better kept small and configuration-oriented, because broad
flexibility and deeply nested structures can become difficult to reason
about.

10
00:01:07.363 --> 00:01:09.470
Fail-safe error handling matters.

11
00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:19.234
Malformed, oversized, unsupported, or resource-intensive input should not
crash the service, leak internals, leave partial state, or create
confusing retries.

12
00:01:19.234 --> 00:01:26.821
Good parser error paths return controlled messages, record useful
operational events, and avoid treating failure as success.

13
00:01:26.821 --> 00:01:32.440
Robust parsing makes the system predictable when inputs are ugly, not only
when they are ideal.
